  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2022 handbook is showing early scholarly uptake and clear usage: it has been cited 3 times in OpenAlex and has recorded 2,167 downloads through OAPEN-based metrics. The citation trail is small but focused, with later articles in public administration and education building directly on the book\u2019s accreditation and program-quality agenda.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The Handbook for Ethiopian Public Administration Program Accreditation addresses a practical and policy-relevant problem: how to improve, assure, and accredit public administration education and training in Ethiopia. Its impact record suggests that the book is being used both as a reference point for scholars and as a resource with sustained reader interest. The most immediate signal is usage, with 2,167 downloads, indicating that the volume has reached a substantial audience beyond its initial publication.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly uptake is still modest but clearly emerging. OpenAlex records 3 citations, all from journal articles published in 2025 and 2026. Those citing works appear in venues such as International Review of Administrative Sciences, Public Administration and Development, and Journal of Public Affairs Education, which places the book\u2019s influence within the core international literature on public administration education, governance competencies, and performance assessment. The citing articles are not broad in number, but they show that the book is being used in research that closely matches its subject matter.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding record also points to a collaborative and institutionally supported project. The book lists multiple authors and was supported by KU Leuven, VLIR-UOS (Belgium), Ambo University, Addis Ababa University, and the Ethiopian Public Administration Association. Open access availability through repository and publisher channels likely contributes to the observed download activity and helps explain how the book has reached readers across academic and practitioner communities.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, this is an early-impact book with stronger evidence of readership than citation volume. The evidence shows practical relevance in a specialized field, active use through downloads, and initial scholarly incorporation into later work on public administration education and accreditation. The record supports describing the book as an accessible and influential reference in an emerging Ethiopian and international conversation about the quality of public administration programs.</p>",
